Source package: at-spi2-core

atk-bridge-cancel-pending-registration.patch
atk-bridge-forget-processed-registration.patch
    Backport of the fix of https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/at-spi2-core/-/issues/234, commits f9776788
    and ce75b1ff of the gnome-50 branch, which at-spi2-core 2.60.6 includes, while Ubuntu 26.04 ships
    2.60.4. atk-bridge, the accessibility bridge of GTK 3 programs, registers a program with the
    accessibility registry with the Embed call, whose reply could be processed after deactivate_bus()
    closed the connection to the accessibility bus, as it does when the bus goes away at the end of the
    session. register_reply() then called get_registered_event_listeners(), which passed the connection,
    NULL by then, to dbus_connection_send_with_reply(), on which libdbus aborts the process. The process
    of Desktop Icons NG, started at the login, aborted this way when the session ended while it was
    still starting, in 2 of about 1970 logins of the session tests, both of them of global-menu-login,
    which logs out a few seconds after the login. The first patch keeps the pending call and cancels it
    in deactivate_bus(). The second, for https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/at-spi2-core/-/issues/240,
    forgets the pending call once its reply was processed, without which deactivate_bus() cancelled a
    pending call which was freed already. Drop both once Ubuntu ships at-spi2-core 2.60.6 or newer.

    The patches add no test, and upstream added none either. The abort needs the reply to be processed
    after deactivate_bus() and before the connection is gone. Stopping the registry with SIGSTOP while a
    GTK 3 window registered, and then the accessibility bus, did not arrange that: the registry ended
    with the bus, and no reply was processed. The build runs at-spi2-core's own tests.
